{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Because that Abraham hearkened to My voice.</b> From here our Rabbis of blessed memory proved that Abraham recognized his Creator at three years of age, for he lived 175 (קע״ה) years, and from when did he hearken to His voice? From 172 (עק״ב) years. If so, at three years of age he recognized Him." "<b>And kept My charge, My commandments, My statutes, and My laws.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat), this refers to commandments that the sons of Noah were commanded: 'My commandments' are robbery and bloodshed; 'My statutes' are a limb from a living animal, and the crossbreeding of cattle and the grafting of a tree; 'and My laws' are civil laws and idolatry — for all of these the sons of Noah were commanded, and he was one who kept and did the will of his Creator, observing even the fine points and stringencies in their commandments, and as our Rabbis of blessed memory said in tractate Avodah Zarah, that Abraham our father had four hundred chapters. And 'My charge,' which is mentioned first, refers to the secondary prohibitions of the laws of forbidden relations for the sons of Noah. But the opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory is that he kept the entire Torah before it was given, and fulfilled the 613 (תרי״ג) commandments by reason, and this is what is said: 'My commandments, My statutes, and My laws' — for the 613 (תרי״ג) commandments are divided into three parts: light commandments received by tradition, which are called testimonies (עדות); and commandments that are decrees of the King and for which we have no reason, which are called statutes (חקים); and commandments grasped by reason, which are called ordinances (משפטים). And this is the language of the verse (Deuteronomy 6:20): ״מה העדות והחקים והמשפטים״ — 'What are the testimonies and the statutes and the ordinances'; also the language of the verse here: 'My commandments, My statutes, and My Torah' — for the ordinances (משפטים) are called Torah; and furthermore, 'My laws' (תורותי) includes the Written Torah and the Oral Torah." "<b>And so</b> say our Rabbis of blessed memory: 'Abraham our father kept even the ordinances of 'eruv tavshilin and 'eruv tehumim.' And so they said concerning the verse (Genesis 45:21) ״ויתן להם יוסף עגלות״ — 'And Joseph gave them wagons' — which they expounded from the passage of the heifer whose neck is broken (עגלה ערופה), that he was engaged in Torah. And the meaning of 'My charge' (משמרתי) would then be: the observance of the Sabbath." "<b>And according to this</b> opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory there is reason to wonder: since the Patriarchs kept the entire Torah before it was given, how did Jacob marry two sisters during their lifetimes, and erect a monument (מצבה), and Amram marry his aunt, and Moses our master, peace be upon him, erect twelve monuments? And the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote that the essential performance of the commandments is in the Land, and the Patriarchs who observed them before the Torah was given — in the Land they did so, but not outside the Land. And Jacob, outside the Land, married two sisters; and likewise Amram married his aunt outside the Land — for the commandments are the law of the God of the Land. And the monument (מצבה) is a commandment that was newly established at a known time. And as for Joseph observing the Sabbath in Egypt, which was outside the Land — it is because the Sabbath is weighed against all the commandments and it testifies to the creation of the world."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :register {:flags {3 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:25.348434679Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}